die@spammer.die (Mark Healey) writes:

[about OS/2 compilation]

> Here is the last screen of output.  I'm wondering what all the
> X11_GRAPHICS stuff is for.  Anyway it looks like some file isn't being
> included that should be.  Do you know what is wrong?

The X11 interface hasn't been tested on OS/2 even by the devteam, and it
probably doesn't work. I think I have heard about some X11 desktop that
can be used to replace the OS/2 PM, but I haven't tried it myself. OS/2
already has about the only graphical interface I have ever seen that I
like, I don't want to replace it with X11 (even though I have X11 on
Linux).

So, I think you probably have #define X11_GRAPHICS in your
include/config.h. Comment it out, and check that #define TTY_GRAPHICS
above it is uncommented.
Commenting out X11_GRAPHICS should lose most of the error messages you
mentioned.

> PM -DX11_GRAPHICS             -Ie:/xfree86/include -Zmtd -o 
> \tmp\obj\unix.o pcun
> ix.c "
> pcunix.c: In function `getlock':
> pcunix.c:123: `errno' undeclared (first use in this function)
> pcunix.c:123: for each function it appears in.)
> pcunix.c:123: `ENOENT' undeclared (first use in this function)

I saw the same error messages when I added some #defines to
include/os2conf.h to enable the directory definitions.
(see http://www.netsonic.fi/~walker/nethack.html#directories )
For that, you need the errno.h header. The include is is in pcunix.h, but
it is conditionalized with 
#if defined(WIN32) || defined(MSDOS)

So, change that to 
#if defined(WIN32) || defined(MSDOS) || defined(PC_LOCKING)
(or something else that enables the #include directive. But the references
 to the missing stuff are within #ifdef PC_LOCKING blocks, so I think this
 is the most sensible fix.)
I also changed another similar condition just a few lines lower.

